---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import db from '../../data/power_stations.json';
import { fullName, metaFromParts, pickTitle, kgToLbs, dollarsPerWh, GUIDE_URLS } from '../../lib/utils.js';
import { STARLINK_LOADS, hoursOnLoad, formatSpan } from '../../lib/guides.js';

const stations = [...db.power_stations].sort((a, b) => a.capacity_wh - b.capacity_wh);
const count = stations.length;

const rows = stations.map((s) => ({
  s,
  hours: Object.fromEntries(STARLINK_LOADS.map((l) => [l.key, hoursOnLoad(s, l.watts, l.efficiency)])),
}));

const tiers = [
  { need: 8, key: 'std_ac', label: '8-hour workday, Standard Gen 3 (AC)' },
  { need: 24, key: 'std_ac', label: 'Full 24 hours, Standard Gen 3 (AC)' },
  { need: 8, key: 'mini_dc', label: '8-hour workday, Starlink Mini (USB-C / DC)' },
  { need: 24, key: 'mini_dc', label: 'Full 24 hours, Starlink Mini (USB-C / DC)' },
  { need: 12, key: 'snow_ac', label: '12 hours with Snow Melt enabled (AC)' },
].map((t) => ({ ...t, pick: rows.find((r) => r.hours[t.key] !== null && r.hours[t.key] >= t.need) }));

const usb100Models = stations.filter((s) => s.usb_c_max_w >= 100);
const dayStd = rows.filter((r) => r.hours.std_ac >= 24);
const cheapestDayStd = [...dayStd].sort((a, b) => a.s.msrp_usd - b.s.msrp_usd)[0];

const title = pickTitle([
  `Power Station for Starlink: ${count} Models (Mini & Gen 3)`,
  'Power Station for Starlink: Runtimes & Sizing',
]);
const description = metaFromParts([
  'Starlink Mini averages ~25W; Standard Gen 3 draws ~55W (110W with snow melt).',
  `Exact runtimes for all ${count} verified power stations on DC and AC, plus solar sizing.`,
]);

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Article',
      headline: 'How long will a power station run Starlink?',
      description,
      url: new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site).href,
      author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'WattLibrary' },
      publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'WattLibrary' },
      datePublished: '2026-11-26',
      dateModified: db.updated,
    },
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Guides', item: new URL('/guides/', Astro.site).href },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Power station for Starlink' },
      ],
    },
  ],
};

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How many watts does Starlink actually use?',
    a: 'Starlink Mini averages 20–30W (we model 25W). Starlink Standard Gen 3 averages 50–65W in normal weather (we model 55W) and climbs to 100–120W when Snow Melt is turned on. Initial boot and satellite acquisition briefly spike higher for a couple of minutes.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I power Starlink Mini from USB-C without turning on the AC inverter?',
    a: `Yes — and you should. Using Starlink's USB-C to Barrel cable requires a 100W USB-C PD port (20V/5A), which ${usb100Models.length} of the ${count} stations here provide. Skipping the AC inverter saves ~10–15% efficiency plus the inverter's idle draw.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What size solar panel runs Starlink all day?',
    a: 'A Starlink Standard Gen 3 uses roughly 55W × 24h ≈ 1,320Wh per day (about 1,550Wh from the battery through AC). With 4–5 peak sun hours and 78% real-world panel harvest, you need ~400–500W of solar panels to break even daily — whereas Starlink Mini on DC (~630Wh/day) breaks even on a single 200W panel.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will a power station keep Starlink online seamlessly during a grid outage?',
    a: 'Starlink dishes reboot if power drops for more than ~20ms, costing 3–5 minutes to reconnect. Pick a station with 10ms or 20ms UPS passthrough if Starlink is your home internet backup.',
  },
];
---
<Base {title} {description} {schema} ogType="article">
  <nav class="crumbs"><a href="/guides/">Guides</a> › Power station for Starlink</nav>
  <h1>How long will a power station run Starlink?</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Every power station here has more than enough inverter wattage for Starlink. The real question is battery capacity —
    and whether you waste 15–25% of it running an AC inverter for a DC dish. Below are the exact runtimes across all {count} verified models.
  </p>

  <div class="kpis">
    <div class="kpi"><b>25 W</b> Starlink Mini avg</div>
    <div class="kpi"><b>55 W</b> Standard Gen 3 avg</div>
    <div class="kpi"><b>110 W</b> with Snow Melt on</div>
    <div class="kpi"><b>{dayStd.length} of {count}</b> run Gen 3 for 24h</div>
  </div>

  <h2>Why Mini vs Standard Gen 3 cuts your battery size in half</h2>
  <p>
    Starlink Mini integrates the router into the dish and averages roughly 25W once locked onto satellites. Better yet, it accepts
    direct DC power via a 100W USB-C PD cable (20V/5A), letting you leave the power station's AC inverter completely switched off.
    A 288Wh station like the <a href="/power-stations/anker-solix-c300/">Anker SOLIX C300</a> or <a href="/power-stations/ecoflow-river-3-plus/">EcoFlow RIVER 3 Plus</a>
    covers an entire 8–10 hour remote workday on Mini DC.
  </p>
  <p>
    Starlink Standard Gen 3 averages 55W on AC — more than double the Mini once inverter losses are included. Over 24 continuous hours
    that consumes roughly 1,550Wh of rated battery capacity, pushing full-day autonomy firmly into the 2,000Wh class unless you add solar panels.
  </p>

  <h2>Starlink runtimes across all {count} models</h2>
  <p class="muted">Rounded down. Mini DC assumes 95% DC-DC efficiency via USB-C PD (100W+ port required) or 12V step-up cable; AC columns assume 85% inverter efficiency.</p>

  <div class="tbl tbl-sticky"><div class="tbl-scroll"><table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Model</th>
        <th>Capacity</th>
        <th>USB-C Max</th>
        {STARLINK_LOADS.map((l) => <th>{l.label}</th>)}
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {rows.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td><a href={`/power-stations/${r.s.slug}/`}>{fullName(r.s)}</a></td>
          <td>{r.s.capacity_wh.toLocaleString()} Wh</td>
          <td>{r.s.usb_c_max_w >= 100 ? <span class="badge badge-ok">{r.s.usb_c_max_w}W PD</span> : <span class="muted">{r.s.usb_c_max_w}W</span>}</td>
          {STARLINK_LOADS.map((l) => (
            <td>{formatSpan(r.hours[l.key]) ?? '—'}</td>
          ))}
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div></div>

  <h2>Minimum station size by scenario</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><div class="tbl-scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>What you want</th><th>Smallest model that covers it</th><th>Capacity</th><th>Weight</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {tiers.map((t) => (
        <tr>
          <th>{t.label}</th>
          <td>{t.pick ? <a href={`/power-stations/${t.pick.s.slug}/`}>{fullName(t.pick.s)}</a> : '—'}</td>
          <td>{t.pick ? `${t.pick.s.capacity_wh.toLocaleString()} Wh` : '—'}</td>
          <td>{t.pick ? `${kgToLbs(t.pick.s.weight_kg)} lbs` : '—'}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div></div>

  {cheapestDayStd && (
    <p>
      Cheapest station at launch to run a Standard Gen 3 dish for a full 24 hours without solar: the
      <a href={`/power-stations/${cheapestDayStd.s.slug}/`}>{fullName(cheapestDayStd.s)}</a> at ${cheapestDayStd.s.msrp_usd.toLocaleString()}
      (${dollarsPerWh(cheapestDayStd.s).toFixed(2)}/Wh).
    </p>
  )}

  <h2>Keeping Starlink running indefinitely with solar</h2>
  <p>
    Because Starlink is a steady 24/7 load, pairing a 1kWh station with solar panels beats buying a heavy 3kWh battery for multi-day off-grid work.
    A 1kWh unit like the <a href="/power-stations/bluetti-elite-100-v2/">Bluetti Elite 100 V2</a> or <a href="/power-stations/ecoflow-delta-3-plus/">EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus</a>
    accepts 1,000W of solar — enough to recharge a full night of Starlink Standard draw in under two hours of morning sun.
    Check your panel's Voc against your station in our <a href="/calculator/#solar">Solar MPPT Checker</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  {faq.map((f) => (
    <details class="faq">
      <summary>{f.q}</summary>
      <div>{f.a}</div>
    </details>
  ))}

  <p style="margin-top:1.4rem;">
    More guides: <a href={GUIDE_URLS.ups}>Home office UPS</a> · <a href={GUIDE_URLS.solar}>Solar MPPT compatibility</a> · <a href={GUIDE_URLS.cpap}>CPAP</a> · <a href="/calculator/">Runtime Calculator →</a>
  </p>

  <div class="disclosure">
    Starlink draw figures reflect published Starlink specs and field averages (Mini ~25W, Gen 3 ~55W, Snow Melt ~110W). Station specs verified ({db.updated}) from manufacturer documentation — see <a href="/about/">methodology</a>.
  </div>
</Base>
